// Generated by build/gen-framework.mjs from FRAMEWORK.md and SKILL.md. Edit those, then run npm run gen:framework.

export const FRAMEWORK_PLAYBOOK = `# RBX Forge Framework Playbook

Read this before you refactor, migrate or optimize a game, or build a new system. It says how an RBX Forge game is laid out, which Harbor library replaces what, in which order to migrate, and when a step is done. The \`forge-refactor\` skill runs it step by step.

Harbor APIs are listed in section 7. Read the linked page before you use a call you have not used in this project yet. Do not guess an API.

## 1. Rules that always apply

- **The server decides.** Clients send intents ("buy item 12", "tackle toward this direction at this time"), never results (damage, currency, positions of other players, "I hit them"). Check every intent against game state on the server: ownership, distance, cooldown, whether the action is possible right now.
- **A refactor keeps behaviour.** Same gameplay, same numbers, same data. If a change alters behaviour (a fixed exploit, a changed cooldown), list it in your report as a behaviour change.
- **Never touch live data by accident.** Never rename a DataStore, a store name or a key prefix that holds real player data. Test data code with Anchor's Studio options (\`Mock = true\`), never against the live store.
- **Never edit:** \`Packages/\`, \`ServerPackages/\`, \`DevPackages/\` (Wally), generated files (\`src/shared/events/*.luau\`, \`src/shared/Ripple/Server.luau\` and \`Client.luau\`), or vendored Harbor code (\`src/shared/Tide\`, \`src/shared/Lighthouse\`, \`src/server/Anchor\`). Change the schema or the game code instead.
- **Studio-only instances.** Remotes, values and scripts that exist only in Studio (Forge marks them \`*\`) are not in the repo. Do not delete them blindly: list them in the report so the user can remove them in Studio, or delete them through the Studio tools when you have them.
- **Small steps.** One feature per change. After each one, run the checks in section 5 and stop so the user can test in Studio.
- \`--!strict\` on every new or rewritten module. Use \`task.wait\`, \`task.spawn\`, \`task.delay\` and \`task.defer\`, never \`wait\`, \`spawn\` or \`delay\`.

## 2. Project layout

Rojo maps the folders (see \`default.project.json\`). If the root has more than one \`*.project.json\`, the game has several places: read section 8 before anything else.

| Folder | In the game |
|---|---|
| \`src/shared\` | \`ReplicatedStorage.Shared\` |
| \`src/server\` | \`ServerScriptService.Server\` |
| \`src/client\` | \`StarterPlayer.StarterPlayerScripts.Client\` |
| \`src/ServerStorage\` | \`ServerStorage\` |
| \`Packages/\` | \`ReplicatedStorage.Packages\` (Wally, never edit) |

**Features.** A feature lives in up to three places:

- \`src/shared/Features/<Name>/\`: shared config and types.
- \`src/server/Features/<Name>\`: the server Service.
- \`src/client/Client/Controllers/<Name>Controller\`: the client Controller.

Creating the shared folder makes the \`./dev\` watcher scaffold the Service and the Controller. Deleting it deletes them, so never rename the shared folder by hand. Use Forge's rename, which moves all three parts together. A server-only feature lives only in \`src/server/Features\`.

**Lifecycle.** ModuleLoader requires every Service and Controller, calls \`:Init(janitor)\` on all of them, then \`:Start()\` on all of them.

- Connect to other modules in \`Start\`, not in \`Init\`.
- Give every connection and instance a module creates to the \`janitor\` it was given in \`Init\`.

**Harbor in the project.**

| Library | How it is installed | Where it lives | Require |
|---|---|---|---|
| Current | CLI pinned in \`rokit.toml\`: \`current = "Acidgorgon/current@<version>"\` | schema \`events.blink\` at the repo root; generated \`src/shared/events/server.luau\` and \`client.luau\` | \`ReplicatedStorage.Shared.events.server\` / \`.client\` |
| Ripple | CLI pinned in \`rokit.toml\`: \`ripple = "Acidgorgon/Ripple@<version>"\` | schema \`src/shared/Ripple.ripple\`; generated \`src/shared/Ripple/Server.luau\` and \`Client.luau\` | \`ReplicatedStorage.Shared.Ripple.Server\` / \`.Client\` |
| Anchor | copy of the repo's \`src/\` | \`src/server/Anchor\` | \`ServerScriptService.Server.Anchor\` (server only) |
| Tide | copy of the repo's \`src/\` | \`src/shared/Tide\` | \`ReplicatedStorage.Shared.Tide\` |
| Lighthouse | copy of the repo's \`src/\` | \`src/shared/Lighthouse\` | \`ReplicatedStorage.Shared.Lighthouse\` |

- These paths are the defaults for a project that does not have them yet. If the project already keeps a schema or a library elsewhere (another \`.blink\` file, \`tools/current-schema.luau\`, another \`.ripple\` file), keep using it, and do not create a second one.
- Library toggles live in \`src/shared/Modules/LibsConfig.luau\` (\`UseCurrent\`, \`UseRipple\`, ...). Change one key at a time and keep the file's formatting.
- Anchor, Tide and Lighthouse are not on Wally yet. If one is missing, or its folder holds only a placeholder \`init.luau\`, ask the user to add it, or copy \`src/\` from the latest release of \`github.com/Acidgorgon/<Library>\`. Write the version you used in \`.forge/MIGRATION.md\`.
- Ripple's CLI writes its output into a folder named after the schema, next to it. That is why the schema is \`src/shared/Ripple.ripple\`. Run \`ripple src/shared/Ripple.ripple\`; Forge does the same when the schema changes.
- If \`rokit.toml\` still pins \`blink\`, switch it to \`current\`: Forge's **Packages → Tools → Switch**, or edit the line and run \`rokit install\`. Then change \`blink\` to \`current\` in \`tools/dev.sh\` and \`tools/dev.ps1\`, or the dev loop keeps compiling with Blink. Blink rejects \`From: Both\`.

## 3. What replaces what

| In the old game | In Forge | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| \`RemoteEvent\`, \`UnreliableRemoteEvent\`, \`RemoteFunction\`, Blink, ByteNet, Zap, Warp, BridgeNet | **Current** events and functions in \`events.blink\` | typed, batched, validated on the server |
| \`ProfileService\`, \`ProfileStore\`, raw \`DataStoreService\`, a custom \`DataService\` | **Anchor** | session locked; dupe-proof trades, exactly-once receipts |
| Value objects, attributes, \`FireClient\` of whole tables, ReplicaService, Replica, Charm, Reflex sync | **Ripple** states in \`Ripple.ripple\` | sends only changed fields; clients get plain tables |
| Custom character or NPC replication, client lerping of other players, Chrono, manual network ownership tricks | **Tide** | interpolation, ownership and a rewind history are built in |
| Lag compensation, trusting client hit reports | **Tide** \`Tide.at\` on the server | see 4.4 |
| \`os.clock()\` timing prints, custom profilers, analytics wrappers | **Lighthouse** | probes, counters, overlay (F8), player telemetry |
| \`leaderstats\` | keep it only for Roblox's player list; drive it from the server | the real state lives in Ripple |

## 4. Migration order

First, the audit.

- **A game that was built in Studio** may have its scripts copied into \`legacy/<Place>/\`. \`legacy/<Place>/INDEX.md\` lists every script with its Roblox path, and every remote. This copy is read-only and Rojo does not map it: the real scripts still run in Studio. Audit from it, write the new code in \`src/\`, and when a feature is migrated, list the Studio scripts it replaces so the user can delete them. Never edit \`legacy/\`. Delete it only when the user says the migration is finished.
- Read the whole game, every place, and write \`.forge/MIGRATION.md\`. If the file already exists, continue from it. With several places, follow section 8 for how to lay it out.
- List every:
  - remote: \`Instance.new("RemoteEvent")\`, \`:FireServer\`, \`OnServerEvent\`, \`InvokeServer\`, \`*.blink\`;
  - data store or profile library;
  - piece of state mirrored to clients;
  - movement or replication code;
  - polling loop;
  - Studio-only script or remote.
- Group them by feature.
- For each feature, write a checklist in this order: networking, data, state, movement, telemetry, optimization.
- Tick items off as you go, so the next session (any harness) knows where things stand.

Then migrate one feature at a time, in this order.

### 4.1 Networking: Current

1. Declare each remote as an \`event\` (or a \`function\` for a client request that needs an answer) in \`events.blink\`.
   - \`From\`: \`Client\`, \`Server\` or \`Both\`.
   - \`Type\`: \`Reliable\`, or \`Unreliable\` for high-rate data that can be lost, like aim or look direction.
   - \`Call\`: \`SingleAsync\` unless you need more.
   - Use the tightest types: \`u8\`/\`u16\` instead of \`f64\`, ranges (\`u8(1..10)\`), \`string(1..32)\`, bounded arrays (\`Item[..20]\`), enums, structs.
   - Never use \`unknown\` or \`any\` for client data.
   - Add \`Rate\` to anything a client can spam.
2. Replace the call sites:

   | Old | New |
   |---|---|
   | \`remote:FireServer(a)\` | \`Net.Name.Fire(a)\` |
   | \`remote.OnServerEvent:Connect(function(player, a)\` | \`Net.Name.On(function(player, a)\` |
   | \`remote:FireClient(p, a)\` | \`Net.Name.Fire(p, a)\` |
   | \`FireAllClients\` | \`FireAll\`, \`FireList\`, \`FireExcept\` |
   | \`InvokeServer\` | \`Net.Name.Invoke(...)\` on the client, \`Net.Name.On(function(player, ...) return ... end)\` on the server |

   There is no server-to-client invoke: use two events, or one \`From: Both\` event. Method names follow the schema's \`option Casing\`: \`Fire\` and \`On\` with Pascal, the default, and \`fire\` and \`on\` with Camel. Match what \`events.blink\` already uses.
3. Set \`Net.SetInvalidPacketHandler(function(player, reason, detail) ... end)\` once on the server, to log and, where it fits, kick.
4. Keep the game checks in the listener. Current only guarantees the shape of the data, not that it makes sense: check ownership, distance, cooldown and state.
5. Compile (\`current events.blink\`), then remove the old remote. Remotes created in code: delete the code. Studio-only remotes: list them.

### 4.2 Data: Anchor

- **ProfileStore or ProfileService:** change the require to Anchor. Store names, keys and the saved format stay the same, and old and new servers can run side by side. Then replace the hand-written join, load, kick and release code with \`Data:Bind()\`, and the helpers with \`Data:Get\`, \`Data:Await\` and \`Data:Update\`. Keep \`Format = "Compat"\` until the user says every old server is gone.
- **Raw \`DataStoreService\` or a custom format:** do not switch silently. Write a plan in \`MIGRATION.md\`: which store and keys hold the data, how the old value is read once and copied into the Anchor template (in \`Loaded\`, then marked as migrated), and how to roll back. Ask the user before you make it live.
- **Developer products:** one handler for the whole game: \`Data:HandleReceipts({ [productId] = function(data) ... end })\`. Remove every other \`ProcessReceipt\`.
- **Trades between players:** use \`Anchor.Trade\`. **Gifts to offline players:** use \`Data:Gift\`. Never move items with two separate \`Update\`s.
- **The template holds saved fields only.** Compute prices, multipliers and display text from saved fields; do not save them.
- **In Studio,** test with \`Anchor.new(name, template, { Mock = true })\`, or \`Reset\` / \`PlayAs\` / \`View\` / \`NoSave\`. These options are ignored on live servers.

### 4.3 State: Ripple

- Describe the state in \`src/shared/Ripple.ripple\`:
  - \`Scope: Player\` for data only its player sees (inventory, currency);
  - \`Scope: All\` for data everyone sees (match clock, score);
  - \`Scope: Manual\` for a chosen group (a team, a party).
  - Mark fields clients must not see as \`server\`.
- Saved player data: the Ripple fields must match the Anchor template. Connect them once:

  \`\`\`lua
  Data:UseRipple(function(player, profile)
      return Ripple.Club.new(player, profile.Data)
  end)
  \`\`\`

  From then on, \`Data:Update(player, function(club) ... end)\` is saved and replicated.
- **Server:**
  - Write through the state object (\`club.Cash += 10\`), never around it.
  - Use \`Ripple.insert\` and \`Ripple.remove\`, not \`table.insert\` and \`table.remove\`.
- **Client:**
  - \`Ripple.Club.wait()\` or \`.get()\`; \`Ripple.observe(t, "Field", fn)\` to drive UI.
  - No polling loops that read state every frame.
- Remove the value objects, attributes and \`FireClient\` calls the state replaces.
- To share one remote with Current, add \`event RippleSync { From: Server, Type: Reliable, Data: buffer }\` and use \`Ripple.setTransport\` (see the Ripple docs).

### 4.4 Movement and hit checks: Tide

- **Player characters:**
  - Server: \`Tide.start({ players = true })\`. Client: \`Tide.start()\`. Connect \`Tide.added\` before \`start\` if you need it.
  - Roll out with \`lockPlayers = false\` first, then let the user test with several clients and switch to the default lock.
- **Remove** remotes that send CFrames every frame, client-side lerping of other characters, and manual \`SetNetworkOwner\` on characters. Tide interpolates (cubic, with an adaptive delay per player) and keeps the server's copy in step.
- **Server teleports:** \`Tide.handle(character):teleport(cf)\` (\`PivotTo\` also works while \`teleportOnWrite\` is on).
- **NPCs, bots and other moving models:**
  - \`Tide.add(model, "Bot")\`, or a kind from \`Tide.kind(name, opts)\` with its own rate and interest.
  - \`handle:setOwner(player)\` hands one to a player.
- **Hit checks, tackles, melee, lag compensation:**
  - The client sends the time it saw the target: \`workspace:GetServerTimeNow() - Tide.delay(target)\`.
  - The server clamps that time to \`[now - MAX_REWIND, now]\` with \`MAX_REWIND\` at most 0.5 s, gets \`Tide.at(target, t)\` and checks the hit against that CFrame, with a little slack.
  - Never accept a client's own claim that it hit something.
- Connect \`Tide.onSuspicious(function(player, reason) end)\` to the game's logging or anti-cheat.
- **Fast physics objects** (balls, projectiles, vehicles) are a design decision, not a drop-in. Write the options in \`MIGRATION.md\` and ask the user.

### 4.5 Telemetry: Lighthouse

- \`Lighthouse.start()\` on the server and on the client, early in startup.
- Measure each system:
  - \`Lighthouse.bind(RunService.Heartbeat, "Combat.Step", fn)\`;
  - \`Lighthouse.wrap("Ai.Think", fn)\`;
  - or \`probe:begin()\` / \`probe:finish()\` around part of a function.
- **Probe names:** \`System.Stage\`, a fixed set. No ids or player names in names. Do not use the \`Current.\`, \`Ripple.\` or \`Tide.\` prefixes.
- **Never yield between \`begin\` and \`finish\`.**
- Use \`Lighthouse.count\` and \`Lighthouse.gauge\` for rates and sizes.
- Replace timing prints and home-made profilers.

### 4.6 Optimization

Measure with Lighthouse first (F8 overlay, \`Lighthouse.export()\`), change one thing, measure again, and write both numbers in \`MIGRATION.md\`.

**CPU:**
- Replace \`while true do ... wait() end\` loops with events or one \`Heartbeat\` step per system.
- Use one connection per system, not one per object, and iterate plain arrays.
- Cache services and instances. No \`FindFirstChild\` chains, \`GetDescendants\`, \`GetChildren\` or \`GetPlayers\` scans inside per-frame code.
- No \`Instance.new\` or \`:Clone()\` per frame or per hit. Pool parts and effects. Move many parts at once with \`workspace:BulkMoveTo\`.
- Add \`--!native\` only to hot, pure math modules, and only after measuring.

**Memory:**
- Every connection goes to a janitor.
- Clear per-player tables in \`PlayerRemoving\`.
- Disconnect and destroy what a round or match created when it ends.

**Network:**
- Fewer, smaller events: tight Current types, \`Unreliable\` for cosmetic high-rate data.
- Let Ripple send changes instead of resending whole tables.
- Keep effects, sounds and UI animation on the client: the server sends one small event and the client plays it.

**Client:**
- Update UI on change (\`Ripple.observe\`, events), not on \`RenderStepped\`.
- Make effects on the client.
- Make sure the game still works with \`StreamingEnabled\`: use \`Instance?\` in schemas for parts that can be streamed out.

## 5. Definition of done (per feature)

- The schemas compile and the generated files are current: \`current events.blink\`, \`ripple src/shared/Ripple.ripple\`.
- The project builds: \`rojo build <place>.project.json -o <temp>/check.rbxl\` for every \`*.project.json\` at the root, not only the default place.
- Lint and type checks the project has pass: selene, luau-lsp, Lune tests.
- Nothing the feature replaced is still referenced: search for the old remote names, \`DataStoreService\`, \`FireServer\` and \`OnServerEvent\` in its files.
- \`.forge/MIGRATION.md\` is updated.
- The report says:
  - what moved;
  - any behaviour changes;
  - Studio-only instances to delete;
  - what the user should test. Play test with **Clients and Servers** and 2 or more players, and for netcode use **Incoming Replication Lag** in Studio's network settings to test high ping.

## 6. Order for a whole game

1. Audit (section 4).
2. Bootstrap:
   - pin \`current\` and \`ripple\` in \`rokit.toml\`;
   - add Anchor, Tide and Lighthouse;
   - start Lighthouse;
   - create \`events.blink\` and \`src/shared/Ripple.ripple\` if they are missing.
3. For each feature, most important first: 4.1 through 4.5.
4. Optimization pass (4.6) with before and after numbers.
5. Remove dead code and Studio-only leftovers, update \`MIGRATION.md\`, final report.

## 7. Library docs

| Library | Docs | Read first |
|---|---|---|
| Current | https://acidgorgon.github.io/Current/ | guide/getting-started, reference/schema, reference/events, reference/generated-api, guide/security |
| Ripple | https://acidgorgon.github.io/Ripple/ | guide/getting-started, guide/server, guide/client, guide/saving, reference/schema |
| Anchor | https://acidgorgon.github.io/Anchor/ | guide/getting-started, guide/migrating, guide/ripple, guide/trades, guide/studio-testing, reference/api |
| Tide | https://acidgorgon.github.io/Tide/ | guide/getting-started, guide/players, guide/entities, guide/time-and-rewind, reference/api, reference/config |
| Lighthouse | https://acidgorgon.github.io/Lighthouse/ | guide/getting-started, guide/probes, guide/network, reference/api |

Without web access, read the vendored source: each library's \`init.luau\` lists its public API.

## 8. Games with several places

One repo holds every place. Each \`*.project.json\` at the root is one place:

- \`default.project.json\` is the main place;
- the others are named after their place, for example \`race.project.json\` or \`lobby.project.json\`.

Forge's place switcher picks which place Rojo serves, and **Serve all places** serves them all.

### Layout

Read every \`*.project.json\` first. Together they say which folders each place gets. Follow the split the project already uses. If it has none yet:

- **Code every place runs** stays in \`src/shared\`, \`src/server\` and \`src/client\`, and every place file maps it. That covers saving, shared systems, the Harbor libraries and the shared schemas.
- **Code only one place runs** goes in \`places/<Place>/shared\`, \`places/<Place>/server\` and \`places/<Place>/client\`, mapped only by that place's file.
- Before you add a folder, check how ModuleLoader finds Services and Controllers, so place-only features still load.

### Harbor across places

- **Anchor: one save for every place.**
  - Every place uses the same store name, the same key and the same template.
  - Keep the template in one server module that every place maps, for example \`src/server/Data/Template.luau\`.
  - On a teleport, the old server ends the session and the session lock hands the save to the new server. \`Data:Await(player)\` waits for it.
  - Never give a place its own store for data the player carries between places. A place's own progress goes in its own key of the same profile, for example \`Race = { BestLap = 0 }\`.
- **Template changes must work on older servers.** Places are published separately, and old servers keep running until they close, so a player can teleport from an updated main place into an old race server.
  - Only add fields, with defaults.
  - Never rename or remove a saved field, or change its type, without a migration in \`MIGRATION.md\` that the user has approved.
- **Teleport data is not trusted.**
  - The client can forge anything in \`TeleportOptions:SetTeleportData\` and \`player:GetJoinData()\`.
  - Keep what matters (match id, team, entry fee) in the save, or in \`MemoryStoreService\` keyed by the reserved server's access code, and check it when the player arrives.
  - Teleport from the server with \`TeleportService:TeleportAsync\`. On \`TeleportInitFailed\`, retry a few times, then tell the player.
- **Current: one shared schema, plus one per place.**
  - \`events.blink\` holds the events every place uses.
  - A place's own events go in \`<place>.blink\` at the repo root, for example \`race.blink\`. Forge compiles every root \`.blink\` file.
  - Give each place schema its own \`ServerOutput\` and \`ClientOutput\` inside that place's folders, for example \`places/Race/shared/events/server.luau\`.
  - Add \`option RemoteScope = "<PLACE>"\` to the place schema. Two schemas loaded in one place would otherwise create remotes with the same name.
- **Ripple: one schema for the game.** States a place never creates cost nothing. The player state that wraps the Anchor profile must be the same in every place.
- **Tide:** call \`Tide.start\` in each place that has moving things, with that place's own options: \`origin\` at the centre of that place's map, and its own kinds. Vehicles in a race place are a fast-physics design decision (see 4.4): ask the user.
- **Lighthouse:** start it in every place. Shared systems keep the same probe names, so places can be compared.

### Migration and release

- \`MIGRATION.md\` gets a **Shared** section and one section per place.
- Migrate the shared systems first: saving, shared events, the Ripple player state. Then do each place.
- Studio can't teleport between places. Test each place on its own with Anchor's \`Mock = true\`. Ask the user to test teleports in a published test place or a private server.
- The report names the places that need publishing. When the save template, \`events.blink\` or the Ripple schema change, every place that uses them must be published.`

export const REFACTOR_SKILL = `---
name: forge-refactor
description: Refactor, migrate or optimize a Roblox game in the RBX Forge framework (GenTree layout and the Harbor libraries Current, Anchor, Ripple, Tide, Lighthouse). Use when asked to refactor or port a game or feature to RBX Forge or Harbor, replace RemoteEvents, Blink, DataStores or ProfileStore, replicate state, add character interpolation or lag compensation, or optimize a Roblox game.
---

# Forge refactor

1. Read \`.forge/FRAMEWORK.md\` in full, and the project's \`AGENTS.md\`. They are the rules for this job and override your defaults.
2. Read \`.forge/MIGRATION.md\`.
   - If it does not exist, do the audit in FRAMEWORK.md section 4 and write it.
   - With more than one \`*.project.json\` at the root, the game has several places. Audit every place and lay MIGRATION.md out as FRAMEWORK.md section 8 says: shared systems first, then each place.
   - For a whole-game refactor, show the user the plan (places, features, their order, open questions such as raw DataStore formats or ball physics) and wait for a go-ahead before changing code.
3. Pick the next unchecked item in MIGRATION.md, or the feature the user named. Migrate it in the order networking, data, state, movement, telemetry, optimization (FRAMEWORK.md 4.1 to 4.6). Before you use an API call you have not used in this project yet, read its Harbor docs page.
4. Run the checks in FRAMEWORK.md section 5 and tick the item in MIGRATION.md.
5. Report:
   - what moved;
   - behaviour changes;
   - Studio-only instances to delete;
   - what to test in Studio.

   Then stop. Do one feature per run unless the user asks for more.`
